{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · one length turned","loop":40,"ease":"","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.3668,-0.1068,-0.0632,0.1768]},{"t":0.38,"box":[-0.3668,-0.1068,-0.0632,0.1768]},{"t":0.42,"box":[-0.0100000289,-0.130000008,0.410000029,0.139999976]},{"t":0.72,"box":[-0.0100000289,-0.130000008,0.410000029,0.139999976]},{"t":0.76,"box":[-0.369584063,-0.10958431,-0.0604159372,0.252327122]}],"stops":[0.18,0.38,0.56,0.72],"beats":[{"title":"An arm held out","text":"Hold an arm out level and raise it until it points straight up. The arm never changes length, and its hand traces a quarter of a circle.\n\nThat is all a circle is from where you stand: **one length, turning**. No second length, no centre measured out, nothing far away consulted. The essays call that one length *H*."},{"title":"Two shares, and the corner","text":"At every moment the arm's reach has two parts: how far forward (*h*, teal, along the ground) and how far up (*v*, gold). Both are shares of the one arm, between 0 and 1. Their ratio v/h is what the direction reads: without bound near upright, 1 at 45°, 0 straight ahead.\n\nAt 45° the two shares are equal. That is **the corner**, v = h. It needs no measurement and no unit: it is where the arm's own two parts trade places."},{"title":"Two rays, meeting on the circle","text":"Now a chord, and a reader at each end. From *A* point up at an angle θ; from *B* point across at 90° − θ. The two rays always meet on the circle drawn on the chord (Thales), and as θ sweeps, the meeting point runs the whole half circle.\n\n*A* reads v/h, *B* reads h/v, its reciprocal. At the top, where the rays cross square, the two readings are equal: the corner again."},{"title":"One point, read twice","text":"From the middle of the chord, the radius to the same point turns at **twice** the angle: 2θ against θ (Euclid III.20). Ray and radius end at one point, every time.\n\nThe ray from the end is the **inscribed** sweep, which lays the reader's addresses; the radius from the middle is the **central** sweep. They are not two motions but one turn, counted from two pivots."},{"title":"π/2 and 2/π","text":"Unroll the arc the hand travelled. It is π/2 of the arm's length, while the arm's straight reach upward is the whole arm, 1.\n\nRead as arc over reach, the turn is **π/2**; read as reach over arc, **2/π**. Each sweep gets at the straight 1 through the curve, one too long and one too short, and only their product gives it back: **π/2 × 2/π = 1**. It is the reader's own length measured in itself, H in H, and the size of nothing else. [The essay, §7](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/02-one-length-turned.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.6,0.6,-0.6,-0.6],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":2},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":30},{"morph":31},{"morph":32},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":35},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":44},{"morph":45},{"morph":46},{"morph":47},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":20},{"morph":19},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":21},{"morph":48},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":26},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":53},{"morph":54},{"morph":55},{"morph":56},{"morph":57},{"morph":58},{"morph":59}],"morphs":[{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":null,"keys":[{"x":[-0.33,-0.1],"y":[-0.07,-0.07],"r":[0,0],"c":[null,null]}],"fill":null,"inPlace":true,"lag":0,"fx":null,"own":{"parent":-1,"parentAt":null,"follow":null,"look":-1,"lookRef":0,"phase":0,"rate":1,"spin":0,"motion":null,"onto":null,"laps":1,"sta